.TH HD-THEME 1 "October 2026" "hd-desktop" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-theme \- add, delete, list, select and apply the themes of HyprDebian
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-theme \-\-list
.br
.B hd-theme \-\-add
.I file
.RB [ \-\-no\-initramfs ]
.br
.B hd-theme \-\-delete
.I name
.br
.B hd-theme \-\-select
.I name
.RB [ \-\-no\-initramfs ]
.RB [ \-\-apply
.RB [ \-\-no\-reload ]]
.br
.B hd-theme \-\-apply
.RB [ \-\-no\-reload ]
.br
.B hd-theme \-\-cards
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-theme
manages the themes in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/themes ,
see
.BR hd-theme (7),
and applies the selected theme to the desktop of the user who runs it.
.BR \-\-add ,
.B \-\-delete
and
.B \-\-cards
are refused when not run as root.
.BR \-\-list ,
.B \-\-select
and
.B \-\-apply
are run by any user.
.PP
Who runs it decides which theme
.BR \-\-list ,
.B \-\-select
and
.B \-\-apply
work on.
For root, it is the theme of the system, in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json .
For any other user, it is the theme of that user, in
.IR ~/.config/hyprdebian/config.json ,
or the theme of the system while the user has none.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the names of the themes, with
.B *
before the selected one, and open
.BR imv-wayland (1)
on the directory of the cards, starting at the card of the selected theme.
For a user who is not root, the theme of the system is marked as such.
Without a Wayland display, only the names are printed.
.TP
.BI \-\-add " file"
Add the theme in
.IR file .
Its name is the name of the file without
.IR .json :
lower case letters, digits,
.B \-
and
.BR _ .
A theme of that name is replaced.
The theme is checked, and its card is rendered with
.BR hd-theme-card (1);
when either fails, nothing is added.
A member
.B name
in the file is left out.
When the theme replaces the theme of the system, it is selected again for
the system, with the boot splash; users who selected it themselves keep the old copy until they
select it again.
.TP
.BI \-\-delete " name"
Delete a theme and its card.
The theme of the system cannot be deleted.
Users who selected it themselves keep their copy.
.TP
.BI \-\-select " name"
Copy the theme
.IR name ,
with its name, to the key
.B hd-theme
of a configuration with
.BR hd-config-update (1).
Run as root, it becomes the theme of the system, in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
which users without a theme of their own get.
Run as any other user, it becomes the theme of that user, in
.IR ~/.config/hyprdebian/config.json .
Programs that read the theme themselves, such as
.BR hd-menu (1),
use it at once.
The desktops do not change until each user runs
.BR "hd-theme \-\-apply" ;
with
.BR \-\-apply ,
the theme is applied to the desktop of the user at once, after it is
selected.
A user goes back to the theme of the system with
.BR "hd-config-remove hd-theme" .
.IP
The boot splash of
.BR hd-plymouth (7)
uses the theme of the system.
Selected as root, the theme is rendered into the splash with
.BR "hd-plymouth-render \-\-update\-initramfs" ,
which rebuilds the initramfs of all kernels when the splash changed; that
takes a while.
The graphical bootloader of
.BR hd-bootloader (7)
uses it too: when hd-bootloader is installed, the bootloader is written to the
EFI system partitions again with
.IR /usr/lib/hd-bootloader/update-esps .
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Write the theme files of the desktop programs in the home directory of the
user, from the selected theme, and reload the programs whose theme file
changed; see
.B APPLYING
below.
Alone, or with
.BR \-\-select ,
which selects the theme first.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reload
With
.BR \-\-apply :
write the theme files, reload nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-initramfs
With
.B \-\-select
or
.B \-\-add
as root: render the boot splash, but do not rebuild the initramfs nor write
the bootloader.
The next rebuild, such as for a new kernel, takes the new splash and
bootloader.
.TP
.B \-\-cards
Render the cards of all themes again, and remove the cards of themes that are
gone.
.SH APPLYING
.B \-\-apply
makes the theme files from the jinja2 templates in
.IR /usr/share/hd-theme/templates .
Each one is used by the config of its program:
.TP
.I ~/.config/kitty/hd-theme.conf
kitty.conf has
.BR "include hd-theme.conf" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/hypr/hd-theme.lua
hyprland.lua has
.BR "pcall(require, \(dqhd-theme\(dq)" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/hypr/hd-theme-hyprlock.conf
hyprlock.conf sources it and uses its variables
.BR $hd_font ,
.BR $hd_highlight ,
.BR $hd_normal ,
.BR $hd_light ,
.BR $hd_dark ,
.BR $hd_border ,
.B $hd_radius
and
.BR $hd_background .
.TP
.I ~/.config/hypr/hd-theme-hyprpaper.conf
hyprpaper.conf sources it and uses its variable
.BR $hd_background ,
the image of the background that fits the monitors of Hyprland.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mako/hd-theme
The config of mako has
.BR include=~/.config/mako/hd-theme .
.TP
.I ~/.config/hypr/hd-theme-hyprtoolkit.conf
hyprtoolkit.conf sources it, for the dialogs of Hyprland.
.TP
.IR ~/.config/qt5ct/colors/hd-theme.conf ", " ~/.config/qt6ct/colors/hd-theme.conf
The colour scheme of the Qt applications; qt5ct.conf and qt6ct.conf have
.BR color_scheme_path=~/.config/qt5ct/colors/hd-theme.conf
and the same for qt6ct.
.TP
.IR ~/.config/gtk-3.0/hd-theme.css ", " ~/.config/gtk-4.0/hd-theme.css
The named colours of Adwaita and libadwaita for the GTK applications; gtk.css
has
.BR "@import url(\(dqhd-theme.css\(dq);" .
.TP
.I ~/.vscode-oss/extensions/hyprdebian.hd-theme/
An extension with the colour theme HyprDebian of VSCodium;
.I ~/.config/VSCodium/User/settings.json
has
.BR "\(dqworkbench.colorTheme\(dq: \(dqHyprDebian\(dq" .
The colours of the syntax are those of Dark+ of VSCodium, which the theme
includes from
.I /usr/share/codium
when VSCodium is installed there.
When
.I ~/.vscode-oss/extensions/extensions.json
is there,
.B hd-theme
adds the extension to it, as VSCodium only finds new extensions by itself
when it makes that file or while it runs.
.PP
The configs themselves belong to the user and are never changed; the ones
that
.B hd-desktop
puts in
.I /etc/skel
use the theme files.
A config that does not use its theme file is reported.
Settings after the line that uses the theme file take the place of the
theme.
.PP
A theme file is only written when it changes, and only then its program is
reloaded: Hyprland with
.BR hyprctl ,
hyprpaper by a restart of its user service, kitty with SIGUSR1 and mako with
.BR makoctl .
hyprlock reads its config when it starts, VSCodium and hyprtoolkit watch
their theme files, and the GTK and Qt applications take their colours when
they start.
.PP
.B \-\-apply
also sets the font of the GTK applications, of the interface and of the
documents and of the monospaced text, to
.B font
in
.BR font-size-small ,
scaled as below, with
.B dconf write
in the dconf database of the user: the keys font-name, document-font-name and
monospace-font-name of
.IR /org/gnome/desktop/interface .
The Qt applications keep
the font of qt5ct.conf and qt6ct.conf.
dconf needs the session bus; without one, the font is left as it is.
.PP
The sizes in the theme files are those of the theme multiplied by the factor
of the key
.B hd-display
of
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
so the theme looks the same size on every monitor; see
.B SCALING
in
.BR hd-theme (7).
The font sizes are rounded to a tenth of a point,
.BR border ,
.BR radius ,
.B gap
and
.B icon_size
to whole pixels.
The theme in the configuration keeps its own sizes.
When the factor is not 1,
.B \-\-apply
prints it.
After
.B hd-display
changed, each user runs
.B hd-theme \-\-apply
again.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cp /etc/hyprdebian/themes/default.json meadow.json
editor meadow.json
sudo hd-theme \-\-add meadow.json
hd-theme \-\-list
sudo hd-theme \-\-select meadow
hd-theme \-\-apply
.fi
.PP
A theme for one user only, and back to the theme of the system:
.PP
.nf
hd-theme \-\-select sand \-\-apply
hd-config-remove hd-theme
hd-theme \-\-apply
.fi
.PP
A 42 inch monitor seen from 90 cm:
.PP
.nf
sudo hd-config-update hd-display '{"diagonal": 42, "distance": 90}'
hd-theme \-\-apply
sudo hd-launcher-cache \-\-update\-all
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-theme (7),
.BR hd-theme-card (1),
.BR hd-config (7),
.BR hd-config-remove (1),
.BR hd-plymouth-render (8),
.BR imv-wayland (1)
